DTrace ninth expansion: 20 passes through different systems
==========================================================

Scope and result
----------------
Nineteen different runtime components add 40 probe contracts: 24 kernel SDT
and 16 user-space USDT. The 12 kernel source files and eight base utility
sources have zero overlap with the eighth expansion's runtime sources.
Ten bsdinstruments profiles are included in the base-install catalog.
coverage-ninth.json records selectors, types, category/priority, source hashes
and the pass log. The eighth round's whole-tree census guided selection;
this is targeted semantic coverage, not a claim every subsystem is complete.

The new systems are virtual-interface cloning, bridge control, link aggregation,
VLAN configuration, tun/tap blocking reads, tarfs, unionfs, nullfs, ISO9660,
memory-disk management, terminal control, link construction, directory removal,
file flags, process priority, module load/unload tools, sync, and file locking.
Most were missing explicit semantic SDT/USDT sites in the census. Existing
TTY and kernel module lifecycle probes remain; these additions cover separate
control-phase and utility boundaries.

Focused pass log
----------------
 1. Interface cloning: ioctl and Netlink create requests after cloner lookup; no attach-time auto-create site.
 2. Bridge: validated control callback timing and result, outside packet forwarding.
 3. Link aggregation: member add/remove lifecycle and failure before dropping the interface reference.
 4. VLAN: ioctl association with parent/tag and configuration result.
 5. Tun/tap: blocking character-device reads and wake errors, no packets.
 6. Tarfs: backing/compressed stream read latency, requested extent and residual/error.
 7. Unionfs: actual lower-to-upper copy phase timing and error.
 8. Nullfs: initial vnode cache hit/miss and getnewvnode latency/error; no allocator internals.
 9. CD9660: buffer/cluster read extent, latency and error before touching the returned buffer.
11. Memory disks: ioctl attach/detach/resize/query dispatch; excluded preload initialization.
12. Terminal: validated tty ioctl dispatch latency and failure, no terminal bytes.
13. ln/link: hard/symbolic link creation syscall latency/error; no path strings.
14. rmdir: each directory/parent removal attempt and syscall errno.
15. chflags: requested file flags and actual chflagsat outcome, including suppressed failures.
16. renice: selector/identity/requested priority and kernel outcome.
17. kldload: actual load attempt latency, returned module id and errno.
18. kldunload: unload id/flags, syscall result and errno.
19. sync: whole sync syscall boundary; done does not invent an errno for a void API.
20. lockf: fd flock and path open-with-lock operations, including lock release and interruption; no filenames.

Category policy and diagnostic use
----------------------------------
P0 lifecycle/flow and latency use synchronous start/done pairs around concrete
work. P0 errors record the underlying callback/syscall return, with errno
separate for user-space negative returns. A successful inner phase is not
necessarily success of the enclosing ioctl, mount, or application request.
P1 resources/state are covered by nullfs cache outcomes and node acquisition,
link-aggregation membership operations, and requested process priority.
P1 synchronization covers tun/tap sleeps and lockf acquisition/release.
P1 I/O/networking covers filesystem reads/copy-up, virtual-network control,
terminal/device requests and sync. No packet, file or terminal bytes are traced.
P2 security-related changes expose chflags' requested flags and syscall result;
policy decisions remain the responsibility of existing MACF/privilege probes.
No credentials, pathnames, module names or command arguments are collected.

Kernel probe contracts
----------------------
ifclone create-start(cloner)/done(cloner,error) wraps the shared create call in
public ioctl and Netlink request entry points after successful cloner lookup.
The attach-time automatic-interface-creation call site is not instrumented.
A missing cloner fails before the pair. The result includes create/modify work
performed by that call; it does not identify the interface by name.
bridge control-start(bridge,subcommand)/done(bridge,subcommand,error) wraps the
validated control callback. Privilege, length and copyin checks precede it;
post-callback MTU notification and copyout follow it.
lagg port-start(aggregate,member,add)/done(aggregate,member,add,error) brackets
ioctl member add (add=1) or removal (add=0). The referenced interface remains
held until after done. The potentially freed lagg-port object is not traced.
vlan configure-start(interface,parent,tag)/done(interface,parent,tag,error)
wraps ioctl vlan_config after validation while holding a parent reference.
It does not cover unconfiguration or all Netlink modification paths.
tuntap read-wait-start(tunnel)/done(tunnel,error) brackets one blocking device
read sleep. Nonblocking empty-queue EWOULDBLOCK occurs before this pair.
tarfs read-start(mount,raw,offset,length)/done(mount,residual,error) covers the
backing read including range/vnode locking. raw=false selects the compressed
stream only when a compressed vnode exists. This is not a mount-wide pair.
unionfs copyup-start(lower,upper)/done(lower,upper,error) measures actual file
copying after both required opens. Creation/open/metadata/close failures lie
outside this pair. Vnodes remain referenced during the call.
nullfs cache-hit/cache-miss(mount,lower_vnode) reports the initial hash lookup.
A miss may subsequently race with another creator and use its existing vnode.
node-start(mount)/done(mount,error) surrounds getnewvnode only: it excludes
allocation of the nullfs private node, mount-queue insertion and hash insertion.
isofs block-start(vnode,logical_block,size)/done(vnode,logical_block,
size,error) surround buffer/cluster/readahead reads. The pair completes before
accessing a returned buffer. It includes cache-hit time and can represent
readahead beyond the named block; it is not one physical-device I/O request.
The ISO9660 provider is named isofs: DTrace treats a provider ending in decimal
digits, such as cd9660, as a userspace provider with a PID suffix.
mdio control-start(command)/done(command,error) covers attach/detach/resize/query
ioctl dispatch, including compat32 dispatch, after version/argument preparation.
It does not instrument preloaded-memory-disk initialization or inspect an
uninitialized request for an unsupported command. Final response copying is
outside the pair.
tty control-start(terminal,command)/done(terminal,command,error) wraps tty_ioctl
after device admission and command preparation while holding the terminal lock.
Early admission failures and specialized early ioctl routes are not covered.

User-space probe contracts and build integration
-----------------------------------------------
Every utility uses operation-start / operation-done under its provider below.
MK_DTRACE=yes builds provider metadata and guarded sites; MK_DTRACE=no builds
omit the metadata and calls. Headers preserve errno and suppress argument
evaluation while disabled. New providers follow ordinary base build targets:
  linkop       ln/link     start(symbolic), done(symbolic,result,errno)
  dirremove    rmdir       start(0), done(0,result,errno)
  fileflags    chflags     start(flags), done(flags,result,errno)
  priorityop   renice      start(which,who,priority),
                          done(which,who,priority,result,errno)
  moduleload   kldload     start(0), done(0,module_id_or_minus_one,errno)
  moduleunload kldunload   start(id,flags), done(id,flags,result,errno)
  flushall     sync        start(0), done(0)
  lockfile     lockf       start(kind,flags), done(kind,flags,result,errno)

The constant-zero arguments are reserved. linkop symbolic=1 requests a symlink,
0 a hard link; overwrite cleanup and earlier validation are outside the pair.
rmdir emits a pair for each syscall, including -p parents. chflags reports the
actual chflagsat result even when -f suppresses its diagnostic. Requested flags
and priority are not assertions that a kernel policy accepted them.
kldload records EEXIST even when -n subsequently treats it as successful.
kldunload records actual syscall flags, including force. Name resolution and
verbose status lookup precede its pair. Neither tool emits module pathnames.
sync's C API is void; done records return only, not an invented error code or
a guarantee about hardware power-loss durability.
lockf kind=0 wraps flock, kind=1 wraps open with O_EXLOCK. The former returns
0/-1; the latter returns a descriptor/-1. Flags are the respective syscall's
flag domain, not interchangeable. LOCK_UN cleanup also appears under kind=0.
Filename and descriptor identity are deliberately not exposed as lock identity;
profiles diagnose a process's attempts, not cross-process inode ownership.

Ten base-installed profile sources
----------------------------------
  virtualnet-control
  tunnel-terminal-control
  filesystem-read-phases
  stacked-filesystems
  memory-disk-control
  base-link-flags
  module-tool-phases
  priority-changes
  file-lock-operations
  base-sync-duration

Profiles use scalar arguments and per-thread nested timing. Pointers are
opaque identities and never dereferenced. Timing includes scheduling delay.
PID filtering selects executing context; kernel work may run on behalf of a
different originating process. Begin before the workload. Dropped events or
unobserved starts invalidate pairing. All ten sources are selected by the
existing automatic profile Makefile, with 365 catalog entries at validation.

Safety and overhead
-------------------
The new kernel sites use disabled SDT NOPs, compile away without KDTRACE_HOOKS,
and add no allocation, locks, timestamps, formatting or payload copies. Control
operations and actual waits are favored over per-packet instrumentation.
Filesystem block and cache events can be frequent when enabled; select only
needed profiles and filter workloads. Runtime overhead has not been measured.
No new probe was placed in DTrace internals, NMI/IPI/interrupt entry, early
bootstrap, CPU restore, pmap, allocator implementation or memory-disk preload
setup. Filesystem code can execute before tracing becomes available (for
example a root mount); disabled sites cannot capture those earlier events.
Existing interrupt-thread and VM-object probes retain prior documented limits.

Validation
----------
Artifacts: /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-ninth-validation
  before/, session.patch       per-file baseline and only this round's patch
  base-results.json            16 successful builds: eight utilities, on/off
  kernel/results.json          24 successful source compiles: 12 files, on/off
  modules/results.json         ten successfully linked loadable modules
  kernel-link.log              final GENERIC-NODEBUG link, no install
  dof-results.json             eight linked binaries and disabled counterparts
  object-results.json          actual SDT NOP patchpoints and no-hooks absence
  profile-results.json         20 plain/PID-filtered offline compilations
  tests/results.json           98 passed, four root-only cases skipped
  smoke/guard-results.json     16 fault-injected enabled/disabled header checks
  smoke/behavior-results.json  16 intercepted utility success/failure checks
  link-results.json            final kernel/module symbols and hashes
  validation.json              aggregate verification result and patch hash

The existing ln, rmdir, chflags, lockf and renice suites run against this
round's built utilities via PATH wrappers. Only the utility processes use the
matching prepared-world libraries; host ATF tools keep their own libraries.
Root-wide renice tests are skipped; pid/process-group tests create their own
children. No other user's priority is changed. lockf tests exercise acquisition,
contention, timeout, child status and cleanup. Tests use fresh object-root
working directories and per-case timeouts.

One existing chflags test assumed a newly created file had no flags. This
host filesystem creates files with uarch. The test now records the original
flags and requires them to remain unchanged after a rejected external symlink
traversal. It still checks the security behavior and no longer assumes a
particular filesystem's initial flags. Both tracing configurations pass.

The guard test compiles the real trace headers against an artificial backend
that changes errno. Every new macro preserves errno with the backend enabled
and avoids argument evaluation when disabled. This is not live DTrace firing.
The behavior test links actual kldload/kldunload/sync/chflags program objects
against --wrap replacements for only the relevant syscalls. It verifies
success, EACCES, and kldload -n/EEXIST without loading/unloading any module,
changing file flags through the stub, or invoking system-wide sync.

Reproduction from /usr/src with a prepared native amd64 world/kernel and OS
permission to write standard /usr/obj paths:
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-base.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-compile.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-modules.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-check.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-tests.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-guards.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-ninth-smoke.py
  make -C /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG -j16 kernel

The tarfs compile driver supplies the same Zstandard FreeBSD compatibility
include directory as its module Makefile. Parallel builds use all 16 logical
CPUs. These are development validations, not publication worlds/packages.
No repositories moved to a home directory; no packages were installed or
published, modules loaded, interfaces changed, filesystems mounted, services
restarted or machine rebooted. /dev/dtrace is unavailable, so live firing,
actual kernel runtime behavior, hardware and enabled overhead remain unverified.
